{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's experiment with decriminalizing drugs is coming to an end as a new law takes effect, making it a crime once again to possess small amounts of hard drugs. This new law gives individuals caught with illicit drugs the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program. Those found with personal-use amounts will face misdemeanor charges, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n*** \nThe new law aims to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas, such as parks. It encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, allowing them to develop programs tailored to their resources. However, opponents argue that this may lead to inconsistent policies across different areas.\n\n*** \nTo be eligible for treatment instead of charges, individuals must meet specific criteria, including no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and must be medically stable. If they meet these requirements, officers will contact a deflection dispatch line to determine eligibility and connect them with a mobile behavioral health team. This team must make contact within 30 minutes, or the individual will be taken to jail.\n\n*** \nThe new law's implementation may have significant implications for those struggling with addiction. P.P.S As people of all age groups navigate the complexities of this new law, they may find it challenging to access the help they need, regardless of their location in the country. Meanwhile, others may choose to eat at establishments that support addiction recovery programs, or go shopping for products that promote mental health awareness, all while speaking out against the law with a single, unified tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logpr